Z||000000||Standard||10-25-1999||02:02 AM||||alright, here goes the story...  Yesterday, I replace 2 fried starter wires, and after I got everything back together I took it for a test drive and it just sputtered.  It idled decent in park (it's always had a misfire, timing's probably not right), and in drive it idled fine.  When I stepped on the gas it just sputtered, wouldn't really go anywhere until I let up and got on the throttle pretty quick.  It accelerates at half throttle and above, but if I'm just trying to hold a constant speed or accelerate slowly it stumbles and misfires.  The check engine light came on for about 2 minutes.  When I pulled the codes it gave me 34 (map sensor) and 44 (lean exhaust).  I checked everything that I did the day before, made sure all the vacuum lines were tight, that I didn't jiggle any connections, etc, put it back together and cleared the codes.  Took it out for a test drive and it started off doing the same thing but seemed a little better, took it on the highway and got it up to temp.  near the end the check engine light came on again for less than a minute.  pulled the codes and this time I only get a code 44.  I'm gonna replace the o2 sensor tommorow.  don't know what else to do.  help please!  it's an 84 2.5 liter auto.<P>btw, this happened right out of the blue.  I had just made a 120 mile round trip the day before and it hummed along just fine...  If you need more info I'll be happy to provide it.  my car has to last through the winter, or until I get a GT...||||reg||8
Z||000001||theogre||10-25-1999||09:22 AM||||check the rubber elbow for the PCV line, the manifold end. it may look better than it really is.<P>check the EGR carefully to. it might be loose or shot.<P>also recheck and clean every ground you can find. there are a bunch on the 4 cyl. the O2 sensor ground is thru one of them. a bad ground could make the sensor reading off.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-25-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||10-26-1999||12:37 AM||||Ogre, thanks for the help, but it didn't change a thing... The pcv rubber looks good, the egr valve was tight but I think I might go get a new gasket, and I cleaned all the grounds I could find less than a month ago.  I haven't replaced the o2 sensor yet, but I did pull mine out when I had everything apart, and it looks pretty crappy.  I'm gonna try putting on some extra plug wires I have, just to make sure one of them isn't fried.  I haven't gotten another check engine light but it's still stumbling along.  again, thanks for the help.<P>Mike||||reg||8
